const MORE={
prevention:[["Why vaccination matters","Vaccines train the immune system to recognise specific germs before exposure. They protect the individual animal, reduce outbreaks in herds and flocks, and for diseases such as rabies they also protect people. Which vaccines are needed depends on species, age, region and risk, so schedules must come from a local veterinarian or the official animal-health programme."],
["Animal-specific preventive care","&bull; Dogs and cats: vaccination, parasite control, dental checks, weight monitoring<br>&bull; Cattle, buffaloes, goats and sheep: vaccination under official programmes, deworming plan, mineral feeding, clean milking<br>&bull; Poultry: vaccination programme, biosecurity, ventilation, clean litter<br>&bull; Horses: vaccination, hoof and dental care, parasite management"],
["Routine veterinary checkups","Regular examinations find problems early, when they are easier to manage. Keep a simple record of vaccinations, deworming, weights, breeding dates and any illness to share with the veterinarian."],
["Deworming awareness","Worms are common in grazing and companion animals. Deworm on a veterinarian-advised programme based on age, risk and faecal testing. Random or repeated treatment can help parasites become drug resistant."],
["Parasite prevention","Ticks, fleas, mites and flies spread disease and cause irritation. Keep housing dry and clean, manage manure, inspect animals regularly and use only products approved for that species. Some dog products are toxic to cats."],
["Hygiene practices","&bull; Clean feeders and water points often<br>&bull; Dispose of waste and dead animals safely<br>&bull; Wash hands after handling animals<br>&bull; Quarantine new animals before mixing<br>&bull; Separate sick animals early"],
["Preventive care FAQs","Does a vaccinated animal never fall ill? Vaccines greatly reduce risk and severity but are not a guarantee.<br>Do indoor pets need vaccines? Often yes. Some germs arrive on shoes or visitors, and rabies rules may apply by law.<br>Can pregnant animals be vaccinated? It depends on the vaccine, so ask a veterinarian first."]],
welfare:[["Daily animal care","Every animal needs food, clean water, shelter and a daily look-over. Noticing changes in appetite, drinking, droppings, movement or behaviour is the earliest warning of illness."],
["Clean housing and shelter","Housing should be dry, ventilated, free from draughts and extreme heat, with enough space to lie down and turn around. Clean bedding and regular removal of waste reduce disease and parasites."],
["Grooming and hygiene","Brushing, bathing as suited to the species, nail and hoof care, and checking skin, ears and teeth help detect problems early. Over-bathing can harm skin, so follow species-appropriate advice."],
["Exercise and movement","Dogs need daily walks and play, grazing animals need room to move, and horses need regular work or turnout. Too little exercise leads to obesity and behaviour problems."],
["Behaviour","Normal behaviour differs by species. Fear, aggression, repeated pacing, hiding or sudden changes can signal pain, stress or illness. Gentle handling and a predictable routine reduce stress."],
["Environmental enrichment","Enrichment gives animals chances to express natural behaviour: foraging toys for dogs, scratching posts and climbing places for cats, varied forage for ruminants, perches and dust baths for poultry."],
["Animal welfare principles","The Five Freedoms are freedom from hunger and thirst, from discomfort, from pain, injury and disease, from fear and distress, and freedom to express normal behaviour."]],
reproduction:[["Reproductive health","Good nutrition, correct body condition, disease control and records support fertility. Reproductive problems often show as repeated failed breeding, abnormal discharge or irregular cycles."],
["Breeding management","Plan breeding with a veterinarian. Select healthy breeding animals, avoid breeding too young, keep breeding and vaccination records, and screen for diseases that spread through breeding."],
["Pregnancy care","Provide balanced feed for the stage of pregnancy, keep stress low, avoid sudden diet changes, and arrange pregnancy checks and pre-birth preparation with a veterinarian."],
["Birth and newborn care","Prepare a clean, quiet, dry birthing area. Newborn livestock need warmth, hygiene and early colostrum from their mother, which gives vital first immunity. Watch that every newborn is up and feeding."],
["After birth","Check that the placenta is passed within the normal time for the species, watch for discharge, fever or poor appetite, and monitor milk flow and the mother bonding with her young."],
["Reproductive disorders","Examples include difficult birth, retained placenta, uterine infection, repeated breeding failure and abortion. Diagnosis and treatment must be done by a veterinarian."],
["Veterinary supervision","Call a veterinarian for long unproductive straining, abnormal or foul discharge, a mother in distress, newborns that do not feed, or any abortion, which can also signal an infectious disease."]],
education:[["Veterinary education overview","Veterinary education trains professionals in animal health, livestock production, food safety and public health. It combines classroom learning, laboratory work, clinical training and field exposure."],
["Undergraduate programmes","The professional degree in India is commonly BVSc and AH, usually about five and a half years including an internship. Check the exact duration and structure on each institution website."],
["Postgraduate programmes","MVSc programmes offer specialisation in subjects such as anatomy, physiology, pathology, microbiology, medicine, surgery, gynaecology, nutrition and public health."],
["Doctoral and research opportunities","PhD programmes and funded research projects cover disease diagnostics, vaccines, nutrition, production, biotechnology and One Health."],
["Admission pointers","Entry to undergraduate veterinary programmes in India commonly runs through national or state entrance processes. Confirm eligibility, seats and dates directly with the institution."],
["Career pathways","&bull; Clinical practice and veterinary hospitals<br>&bull; Government veterinary and animal husbandry services<br>&bull; Research and teaching<br>&bull; Dairy, poultry and feed industry<br>&bull; Food safety and public health<br>&bull; Wildlife and zoo health"]],
extension:[["Farmer education","Training on feeding, clean milking, housing, vaccination calendars, recognising disease early and record keeping helps farmers protect animals and income."],
["Livestock health awareness","Health camps and advisories explain vaccination, deworming, mastitis control and when to call a veterinarian. Early reporting of unusual disease helps stop outbreaks."],
["Animal welfare campaigns","Campaigns promote humane handling, transport and slaughter standards, responsible pet ownership and stray animal vaccination."],
["Community veterinary programmes","Mobile clinics, vaccination drives and partnerships between universities, local government and farmer groups bring services close to communities."],
["Rural veterinary outreach","Outreach reaches remote villages with advice, basic treatment and referral. Local veterinary hospitals, dispensaries and Krishi Vigyan Kendras are common first points of contact."],
["How people can take part","Students and volunteers can assist at camps, help with awareness drives and share verified information. Always work under qualified veterinary supervision."]],
onehealth:[["The human-animal-environment link","Human, animal and environmental health are connected. Disease can pass between animals and people, and soil, water and climate affect all three."],
["Zoonotic disease awareness","Examples include rabies, brucellosis, leptospirosis, some parasitic infections and avian influenza. Avoid contact with sick or unknown animals, wash hands, and seek medical and veterinary advice after bites or illness."],
["Food safety","Boil milk before drinking unless it is properly pasteurised, cook meat and eggs thoroughly, keep raw and cooked food apart and maintain clean slaughter and storage."],
["Environmental health","Proper manure, waste and water management reduces contamination, odour and the spread of disease around animal housing."],
["Antimicrobial resistance","Germs can become resistant to antibiotics when they are overused or misused. Use antimicrobials only when a veterinarian prescribes them, never share leftovers and follow the prescribed course."],
["Wildlife conservation","Healthy wildlife and habitats support ecosystems. Wildlife disease surveillance helps protect livestock, people and endangered species."],
["Practical steps","Vaccinate pets and livestock, practise hygiene, report unusual illness early, handle food safely and use medicines responsibly."]]};
const EXTRA={foods:["Feeding basics",[["Feeding principles","Match the diet to the species, age and purpose. Ruminants and horses depend on forage, dogs and pigs are omnivores, and cats need meat-based nutrition. Change feeds gradually over several days."],["Water and hydration","Clean fresh water must always be available. Reduced drinking or sudden thirst is a reason to consult a veterinarian."],["Safe feeding","Store feed dry and clean, discard mouldy feed, and keep human foods that are toxic to animals out of reach."]]],
treatments:["Diagnosis, care and emergencies",[["Common diagnostic methods","Clinical examination, blood tests, faecal and urine tests, skin scrapings, X-ray and ultrasound, and laboratory tests such as culture or PCR."],["Supportive care","Rest, shade or warmth, clean dry bedding, access to water, isolation of infectious cases and feeding support only as advised by a veterinarian."],["Recovery and monitoring","Follow the veterinarian instructions, complete any prescribed course, and track appetite, drinking, droppings, activity and temperature if advised. Attend recheck visits."],["When urgent veterinary attention is needed","Breathing difficulty, collapse, seizures, severe bleeding, bloated abdomen, repeated vomiting or diarrhoea, inability to stand or pass urine, difficult birth, suspected poisoning or any bite from a possibly rabid animal."]]],
diseases:["Protecting animals and people",[["Recognising illness early","Watch for reduced appetite, weakness, fever, discharge, cough, diarrhoea, lameness or behaviour change."],["Isolation and biosecurity","Separate sick animals, restrict visitors, clean equipment and avoid sharing feeders between groups."],["Zoonotic precautions","Wear gloves where appropriate, wash hands, do not handle suspected rabid animals, and report serious or unusual disease to the veterinarian and animal-health authority."]]],
research:["Understanding veterinary research",[["Why it matters","Research improves diagnosis, vaccines, nutrition and food safety, protecting animals, farmers and public health."],["From research to the field","Findings become diagnostic tools, vaccines, treatment guidelines and farmer advice delivered through extension services."],["Judging sources","Prefer peer-reviewed journals and official bodies. Check the date and authorship, and be cautious of claims without evidence."]]]};
const SC=[["#570608","#FBEBD0"],["#344B22","#E8EFD8"],["#7A1115","#F7E1E1"],["#667B32","#F3EFD0"],["#35130D","#F1E4D8"]];
